1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,148
Phase is the subtlest of a wave's three properties — and the one modern digital

2
00:00:07,148 --> 00:00:08,647
radio leans on hardest.

3
00:00:08,647 --> 00:00:13,991
Phase is the position of a point within the cycle of a wave, expressed in degrees,

4
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:16,598
zero to 360, or radians, zero to two pi.

5
00:00:16,598 --> 00:00:22,007
Two waves of the same frequency can differ in phase, meaning one is shifted in time

6
00:00:22,007 --> 00:00:26,895
relative to the other: a quarter-cycle lag is a 90-degree phase difference.

7
00:00:26,895 --> 00:00:32,174
It doesn't change a wave's strength or its pitch — just where it is in its cycle.

8
00:00:32,174 --> 00:00:34,194
And that's enough to send bits.

9
00:00:34,644 --> 00:00:36,943
The everyday anchor is a clock face.

10
00:00:36,943 --> 00:00:42,180
A wave cycling is a hand sweeping around a dial — one lap per cycle, and the speed

11
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:44,096
of the sweep is the frequency.

12
00:00:44,096 --> 00:00:46,715
Phase is where the hand points right now.

13
00:00:46,715 --> 00:00:51,888
Two clocks can tick at exactly the same rate and still disagree: one reads twelve

14
00:00:51,888 --> 00:00:53,676
while the other reads three.

15
00:00:53,676 --> 00:00:56,614
Same frequency, ninety degrees of phase apart.

16
00:00:56,614 --> 00:01:01,596
Phase is only meaningful relative to a reference — another wave, or a notional

17
00:01:01,596 --> 00:01:04,087
clock ticking at the carrier frequency.

18
00:01:04,537 --> 00:01:09,668
That clock isn't just a metaphor — it's literally how radios represent signals.

19
00:01:09,668 --> 00:01:14,930
On the IQ plane, a sample's angle measured from the positive I axis is its phase,

20
00:01:14,930 --> 00:01:18,178
and its distance from the origin is its amplitude.

21
00:01:18,178 --> 00:01:23,504
A rotating point traces out the wave: constant frequency is steady rotation, and a

22
00:01:23,504 --> 00:01:26,102
sudden change of angle is a phase shift.

23
00:01:26,102 --> 00:01:31,429
Amplitude and phase — the two independent quantities of a bandpass signal — become

24
00:01:31,429 --> 00:01:34,287
the polar coordinates of one complex number.

25
00:01:34,737 --> 00:01:40,118
Here's why that matters: phase can be changed instantly and read out precisely,

26
00:01:40,118 --> 00:01:43,592
which makes it prime real estate for carrying data.

27
00:01:43,592 --> 00:01:48,224
Phase-shift keying assigns bit patterns to discrete phase positions.

28
00:01:48,224 --> 00:01:50,744
BPSK uses two — zero and 180 degrees.

29
00:01:50,744 --> 00:01:54,968
QPSK uses four, spaced 90 degrees apart — two bits per symbol.

30
00:01:54,968 --> 00:02:00,349
The receiver just measures the angle of each symbol and decides which was sent.

31
00:02:00,349 --> 00:02:06,003
And differential schemes like pi-over-four D-QPSK go one cleverer: they encode data

32
00:02:06,003 --> 00:02:11,452
in the change of phase between successive symbols, sidestepping the need to know

33
00:02:11,452 --> 00:02:13,904
the absolute phase reference at all.

34
00:02:14,354 --> 00:02:19,755
The catch: the receiver's idea of "zero phase" must be locked to the transmitter's,

35
00:02:19,755 --> 00:02:22,032
or the whole constellation rotates.

36
00:02:22,032 --> 00:02:27,433
A Costas loop or a phase-locked loop estimates and removes that offset continuously

37
00:02:27,433 --> 00:02:29,190
— this is carrier recovery.

38
00:02:29,190 --> 00:02:34,591
And real oscillators jitter, smearing each symbol's angle into a fuzzy cloud called

39
00:02:34,591 --> 00:02:39,992
phase noise; too much of it collapses the margin between adjacent points and forces

40
00:02:39,992 --> 00:02:40,448
errors.

41
00:02:40,448 --> 00:02:45,653
Symmetric constellations even allow locking 90 or 180 degrees off — differential

42
00:02:45,653 --> 00:02:49,948
coding or a known sync pattern resolves which rotation is correct.

43
00:02:50,398 --> 00:02:55,159
The digital voice signals GopherTrunk decodes all live in the angle of the IQ

44
00:02:55,159 --> 00:03:00,292
samples, so its carrier-recovery loop estimates the incoming phase and rotates each

45
00:03:00,292 --> 00:03:04,435
symbol back onto the ideal constellation before slicing it to bits.

46
00:03:04,435 --> 00:03:09,320
Watch the live decode panel on a marginal signal: residual phase error directly

47
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:13,896
raises the error-vector magnitude, and past a point, it breaks the decode.

48
00:03:13,896 --> 00:03:17,420
When symbols garble, phase is usually where the story is.

49
00:03:17,870 --> 00:03:23,132
So: phase is where a wave sits in its cycle, it lives as the angle of every IQ

50
00:03:23,132 --> 00:03:28,595
sample, and deliberately jumping it between fixed positions is how digital radios

51
00:03:28,595 --> 00:03:29,270
send bits.

52
00:03:29,270 --> 00:03:31,091
Full write-up linked below.

